# RKE2's CNI is canal: Calico for policy and the pod veths, Flannel for the
# VXLAN dataplane on flannel.1. Three of its defaults are wrong here, and all
# three fail quietly.
#
# Despite its path in this tree, this file is NOT part of the elemental image.
# It is written by per-node Ignition straight into RKE2's own
# /var/lib/rancher/rke2/server/manifests, on servers only. The why -- a measured
# 104-second race that this file used to lose -- is in locals.tf, at
# node_server_manifests.
#
#  1. flannel.iface defaults to blank, which the chart documents as "the
#     interface is chosen using the node's default route". On a vpc_only
#     control plane that is the VPC NIC and comes out right by accident. On a
#     dual-NIC node -- every bare metal GPU worker -- the default route is the
#     PUBLIC NIC, so flannel annotated
#     flannel.alpha.coreos.com/public-ip with the node's public address and the
#     control planes put it in their FDB that way. Every pod packet between a
#     GPU node and a control plane then leaves the VPC: out through the NAT
#     gateway, across the public internet, back in on a NIC that has no Vultr
#     firewall, as cleartext VXLAN on udp/8472.
#
#     99-node-ip.yaml does NOT prevent this. It pins kubelet's node-ip, which
#     flannel never reads -- it picks its own VTEP source independently.
#
#     regexIface rather than iface because the value is cluster-wide and the
#     NIC names are not: enp1s0 on the cloud control planes, enp1s0f1np1 on
#     bare metal. flannel's --iface-regex matches "IP or name" (flannel
#     main.go:122), so matching the VPC address range picks the right NIC on
#     every node without naming any of them.
#
#  2. calico.vethuMTU defaults to 1450, which assumes a 1500 underlay. Vultr's
#     VPC is 1450, so the VXLAN payload ceiling is 1400 and a pod handed a 1450
#     veth overflows its own node's flannel.1 before the packet leaves the
#     host.
#
#  3. calico.felixPrometheusMetricsEnabled defaults to true, which binds Felix's
#     metrics endpoint to 0.0.0.0:9091. Harmless on the vpc_only control planes,
#     but a bare metal GPU worker has a public NIC and no Vultr firewall behind
#     which to hide it: nmap from off-cluster reports 9091/tcp open, owned by
#     calico-node. Nothing in this stack scrapes it.
#
# Bouncing canal is required to pick any of these up on a RUNNING cluster: the
# values land in the rke2-canal-config ConfigMap, which the DaemonSet reads
# through env.valueFrom, so the pod template never changes and Helm has nothing
# to roll. Irrelevant on a fresh build -- Ignition puts this on disk before RKE2
# first starts -- but it is why a hot patch looks like a no-op.
#
# vethuMTU also only applies to veths created after the change, so on a running
# cluster existing pods keep the old MTU until they are recreated.
apiVersion: helm.cattle.io/v1
kind: HelmChartConfig
metadata:
  name: rke2-canal
  namespace: kube-system
spec:
  valuesContent: |-
    flannel:
      regexIface: '${iface_regex}'
    calico:
      vethuMTU: ${veth_mtu}
      felixPrometheusMetricsEnabled: false
